### Phase 0 — Path & port hardening

Make every fs root and bound port honor environment variables. Process-level multi-tenancy depends on it.

**Tasks:**
- [ ] Create `getDataRoot()` utility. Likely lives in `packages/shared/paths/` (new) or an existing `@slayzone/shared` package — check first.
- [ ] Replace ~12 sites of `process.env.SLAYZONE_DB_DIR || app.getPath('userData')` with `getDataRoot()`.
- [ ] Fix hardcoded `Extensions/` and `browser-extensions.json` paths (browser domain).
- [ ] Audit `app.getPath()` call sites. Electron-only packages may keep them; any code destined for the server package must not depend on Electron.
- [ ] Confirm `SLAYZONE_PORT`, `SLAYZONE_MCP_PORT` honored at every bind site. Add `SLAYZONE_HOST` (default `localhost`).
- [ ] Smoke test: run two `pnpm dev` instances side-by-side with different `SLAYZONE_DB_DIR` + `SLAYZONE_PORT`. Verify zero data crossover.

**Done criteria:** can spin up two isolated dev instances on one machine.

### Phase 1 — Domain extraction (decouple from Electron)

Lift main-process logic into framework-free packages. Same code must be runnable from Electron main *or* from a plain Node process.

**Tasks:**
- [ ] **Convex audit (do first)**: the repo contains a `convex/` directory. Confirm whether Convex is still in active use, and if so whether the server pkg needs to talk to Convex too or whether Convex code is dormant / deprecated. **Investigate the code; ask the user if uncertain.** This determines whether `@slayzone/server` has a cloud dependency or is fully self-contained.
- [ ] Per-domain audit. For each `packages/domains/*/src/main/`, list every Electron import (`app`, `BrowserWindow`, `ipcMain`, `webContents`, etc.).
- [ ] Replace `app.getPath()` with `getDataRoot()` (Phase 0 dep).
- [ ] Replace `BrowserWindow.webContents.send()` with an event-emitter abstraction. Server code emits events; Electron-main wraps as tRPC subscriptions, the renderer subscribes via tRPC client. No more direct `webContents.send`.
- [ ] Move pure logic into `packages/domains/*/src/server/` (or rename `main` → `server` and keep Electron-specific glue in a thin wrapper). Decide naming — check existing convention.
- [ ] `packages/domains/auto-updater/` stays Electron-only.

**Done criteria:** every domain's logic compiles and runs in a plain Node process. Unit tests run without booting Electron. Convex relationship is documented.

### Phase 2 — tRPC transport layer (rip-per-handler)

Build tRPC router. Replace IPC handlers in-place with tRPC procedures. Delete IPC code as you go — no compat layer.

**Tasks:**
- [ ] Add tRPC dependencies. Server: `@trpc/server`. Client: `@trpc/client`, `@trpc/react-query`. WebSocket: `ws` + tRPC's WS adapter.
- [ ] Create `packages/shared/transport/` (or `packages/shared/trpc/`) with: root `appRouter`, per-domain sub-routers, context type, WS server adapter, type-only client export.
- [ ] WebSocket server inside Electron main (Phase 4 will move it to standalone `@slayzone/server` pkg too).
- [ ] **Per IPC handler**: write tRPC procedure (query/mutation/subscription) → migrate renderer call site → delete IPC handler in same PR. One handler at a time, no big-bang.
- [ ] Streaming events (PTY data, agent chat deltas, `tasks:changed`) → tRPC subscriptions over WS.
- [ ] Asset upload procedure: enforce **100 MB per-file limit**, configurable via env (`SLAYZONE_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES`).
- [ ] tRPC procedures are type-imported by renderer — no zod registry needed; tRPC + TS infer end-to-end.

**Done criteria:** `ipcMain.handle` and `webContents.send` are gone (or at least IPC channel count is decreasing PR-by-PR until zero). Renderer talks tRPC over WS to localhost.

### Phase 3 — `@slayzone/server` package (publishable)

Same code, different entry point. Embedded server (Phase 2) gets a thin standalone wrapper.

**Tasks:**
- [ ] New package: `packages/apps/server/`. Add to pnpm workspace.
- [ ] Add to `electron.vite.config.ts` `externalizeDepsPlugin({ exclude: [...] })` if it gets imported by the Electron main bundle (per memory: missing this breaks runtime).
- [ ] `bin/slayzone-server.js` — npm bin entry.
- [ ] `src/index.ts` — boot: bind port (`SLAYZONE_PORT`, default ?), bind host (`SLAYZONE_HOST`, **default `localhost`**, opt-in `0.0.0.0`), init DB at `getDataRoot()`, start MCP, mount tRPC over WS, print banner.
- [ ] **Bundle MCP on the same WS server** as tRPC (single port, multiplexed). CLI / agent CLIs discover MCP URL from Electron settings (or env when running on the server box itself).
- [ ] **Migrate Express local API wholesale** (`POST /api/notify` etc., currently in Electron main) into the server package. No HTTP endpoint stays behind in Electron.
- [ ] **Agent CLI presence check at boot**: server probes PATH for required CLIs (claude, codex, gemini, etc.). **Fail loud and exit non-zero** if missing rather than discovering later in a session.
- [ ] Banner must include: bound URL, data dir, version, **trusted-network warning**.
- [ ] Lifecycle: handle SIGTERM/SIGINT — drain WS, kill PTYs, close DB, exit clean.
- [ ] Config surface: `SLAYZONE_PORT`, `SLAYZONE_DB_DIR`, `SLAYZONE_HOST`, `SLAYZONE_MCP_PORT`, `SLAYZONE_WORKTREE_ROOT`, `SLAYZONE_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES`. CLI flags as alternative to env.
- [ ] Single-writer guard: lockfile in data dir, refuse boot if held. Prevents Electron-main + standalone server from corrupting same DB.
- [ ] Publishing config — keep unpublished initially (`"private": true`) until ready.
- [ ] Cross-platform sanity check: macOS, Linux, Windows. Node 20 + 22.

**Done criteria:** `cd packages/apps/server && node bin/slayzone-server.js` boots, `/health` returns 200, data dir respected. Identical behavior to embedded mode (same backing code).

### Phase 4 — Electron client retarget (local + remote modes)

Add the "Server URL" setting and a Local-vs-Remote toggle.

**Tasks:**
- [ ] Add react-query (TanStack Query) to renderer via `@trpc/react-query`. Memory note: `useTasksData` exposes `window.__slayzone_refreshData` — keep this contract working (or migrate callers).
- [ ] tRPC client points at WS URL. Local mode: `ws://localhost:<embedded-port>`. Remote mode: user-entered URL.
- [ ] **Local mode** behavior: Electron main starts the embedded server in-process (same code as `@slayzone/server` pkg, imported as a lib). Renderer connects via WS to localhost. Server stops when Electron exits.
- [ ] **Remote mode** behavior: Electron main does **NOT** start the embedded server. Renderer connects to user-supplied URL. Health check on connect.
- [ ] **Embedded-server failure handling**: if the in-process server fails to start or crashes in local mode, **auto-restart with exponential backoff**. Surface a user-visible error after repeated failures.
- [ ] Settings UI: "Server URL" field + Local/Remote toggle. Switching modes prompts reload. "Migrate data" wizard visible if switching from local to remote (Phase 5).
- [ ] When in remote mode: hide Electron-only features (browser panel via WCV).

**Done criteria:** Electron app works identically to today in local mode (no perceptible regression). Switching to remote URL works against a `npx @slayzone/server` running elsewhere.

### Phase 5 — Migration wizard (in-app, Electron)

One-way: local data → running remote server. Built into the Electron app, not a CLI command.

**Tasks:**
- [ ] Wizard UI in Electron settings → "Migrate to remote server". Steps: enter URL → health check → preview (counts of tasks/projects/assets/worktrees) → confirm → progress → done.
- [ ] Tar local SQLite (closed cleanly first) + `assets/` + `project-icons/` + `backups/`. Worktrees: complex (often outside `userData`) — decide whether to copy or just rewrite paths.
- [ ] Upload archive to server via tRPC mutation `migrate.import` (streaming). Server validates, imports, returns receipt.
- [ ] Worktree path strategy: server-side worktree root = `getDataRoot()/worktrees/<taskId>/` (overridable via `SLAYZONE_WORKTREE_ROOT`). Migration rewrites `tasks.worktree_path` to match.
- [ ] Dry-run option in wizard: produce a report (what would copy/rewrite/skip) without writing.
- [ ] Integrity check: round-trip checksums on assets, row counts on tables.
- [ ] On success: prompt user to switch to remote mode (Phase 4 toggle).

**Done criteria:** migrate a real local userdata to a remote server, switch to remote mode, all tasks/projects/worktrees present and functional.

