## 11. Deferred — DO NOT ACT, context only

The following sustainability concerns were raised during planning and **explicitly deferred**. They are recorded here so a future session understands the trade-offs were considered, not missed. **Do not implement, design, or scaffold anything for these in scope 1.** They will be revisited when scope 2 (or a security incident) forces the issue.

### 11a. PTY persistence across server restart

**Concern**: the core use case is "start agent, close laptop". A server restart (deploy, OS update, crash, OOM) kills all running PTYs and therefore all in-flight agent work. This directly contradicts the product goal.

**Why deferred**: tmux-style detached PTYs are a large complexity add (process supervision, scrollback persistence, reconnect/replay protocol, ownership re-attachment). Scope 1 ships with the same volatility as today's Electron app.

**Mitigation today**: none. Restart = lost work. Document this loud in the server boot banner.

**When to reconsider**: first user complaint, or before any "long-running headless agent" marketing. At that point we'll need: persistent PTY layer (likely a node-pty wrapper that survives parent exit, or genuine tmux integration), scrollback persistence, reconnect protocol over tRPC subscription. Plan for it then; do not scaffold now.

### 11b. Auth — trusted-network footgun

**Concern**: scope 1 ships with no auth. The intended deployment is "user runs `npx @slayzone/server` on their LAN/VPN". In practice some users will put it on a VPS without a firewall, and any unauth port hit = full RCE on the box (PTY ≈ shell). This is a credible security incident waiting to happen.

**Why deferred**: full auth (login, sessions, OAuth) is weeks of work and orthogonal to "make the backend remotable". Even a shared-secret token (`SLAYZONE_TOKEN` env, sent as WS connect param) is a half-day add but the user explicitly chose to defer all auth for scope 1.

**Mitigation today**: a loud trusted-network warning banner on server boot. Documentation must repeat the warning anywhere the server URL is configured.

**When to reconsider**:
- The first time a user reports their server got hit.
- Before any hosted offering work begins.
- Before the server package leaves `private: true`.

When we add it: shared-secret token first (single env var, ~1 day), then proper sessions later. Adding token auth later **is** a breaking change for early adopters — accept this when the time comes; do not pre-soften it now.

### 11c. Bidirectional migration (remote → local)

**Concern**: scope 1 migration is one-way. Once a user moves their data to a remote server, there is no documented escape if the server dies, the host bills go up, or they want to fall back to local. Backup/recovery path missing.

**Why deferred**: the second direction needs a reverse archive endpoint, conflict resolution if local data is non-empty, and worktree path rewriting in reverse. Doubles the migration test surface. Scope 1 only needs the forward path to deliver the headline feature.

**Mitigation today**: server data dir is just SQLite + folders — a sufficiently motivated user can rsync it back manually. Don't promise this in user-facing docs.

**When to reconsider**: first user request, or before recommending the remote workflow as primary in any onboarding flow.

### 11d. Browser panel feature regression in remote mode

**Concern**: §2 Decisions table specifies that the browser panel UI is hidden when client is remote. This is a feature regression for remote users. Not a sustainability hole the way 11a–c are, but worth recording.

**Why deferred**: full sustainability would be transparent local-render-of-remote-content via proxied URL fetches with cookie sync. This is genuinely complex (CORS, auth headers, file:// URLs, cookies, scroll/zoom state) and not justified by scope-1 evidence of demand.

**Mitigation today**: hide the UI cleanly (no broken affordances), document the limitation.

**When to reconsider**: when remote mode has real users and any of them ask for browser panel parity.

